welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Sarah Brinegar a visiting scholar  who's at Yale as the rice fellow in the  European studies council her research  interests include the history of the  Soviet Union Imperial Russia the late  ottoman empire and imperial germany  today we'll talk with Professor brinegar  about the politics of oil in the  formation of the Soviet Union welcome  professor brinegar Thank You Mara let's  begin with an overview of your book  project tell us about it sure so the  book project is a revision of my  dissertation I'm titling it right now  binding the periphery oil and state  building in the Soviet caucuses and the  project looks at the way that the Soviet  government was dependent on oil in the  weight of that dependency sir led led to  different policy outcomes and shaped the  way that they were able to construct  this state in this early period okay and  I do this sort of at with the actual  military intervention of the the Red  Army into Azerbaijan which is the area  that I focus on and its capital baku  which was the largest oil center in the  russian empire and i look at the way  they rebuild the industry and sort of  all the whole process it goes into this  so the politics that surround it where  you get money where you get capital  resources and how this affects not just  the military campaign but also literally  on the ground and in Baku and also in  the capitals and then I trace it through  its role in foreign policy and the way  that they try to use it to gain  diplomatic ties and different foreign  trade contracts which is not very  successful and then they sort of pivot  and I look at this pivot to a much more  practical on the ground collaborations  with fixers and sort of other some  unsavory types and also setting up a  loyal companies and and the way that  they use these other means to establish  ties okay and what led you to be  interested in this particular topic yeah  that is like most things has has a bit  of a roundabout story I actually started  getting interested in this in this topic  first in Azerbaijan and then in oil they  sort of came separately in 2003 so quite  a while ago now I was in Russia on a  language study and I went to cuz on  which is the capital of an autonomous  republic within the Russian Federation  tatarstan and I went to a school play  and it was in Qatar and I just thought  what what is this or what or what is  this other experience it is a Russia  that didn't know anything about so I  thought I really want to do a project on  the Soviet experience from a non Russian  point of view what was it what was it  like what did it what how was this  different or the same from what was  happening with much more familiar story  and I ended up doing that in my master's  degree I went and looked at the  revolution the Russian Revolution of  1917 from the vantage point of the  Caucasus and from azerbaijan and decided  to pursue that in my PhD and during my  language training for that for us or by  shawnee i was in baku for a summer this  is several years later um and I just  looked around and I saw oil everywhere  and I just thought this is interesting  there's something going on here because  it wasn't just that there was oil and  you there was oil and you it was an oil  city but it seems to be embedded  embedded everywhere and everything was  sponsored by BP or another oil company  it was just really interesting so I  thought I would just sort of do some  background reading and look it up and  kind of then move on with the project  that had been working on and that's not  what happened at all I realized that  there wasn't a lot of information for me  there was almost nothing in English and  even on the 1920s 1930s there was just  startling lea little even in Russian so  I thought oh wow this is this is  something  I can work with I can find out what's  going on okay so you're obviously fluent  in Russian yeah yeah so I was going to  see how so how did you do the  methodology then yeah so it's it's  pretty standard historical archival work  so I did a lot of work first in in  Azerbaijan which was really great where  I got to work with the state oil company  archives and this is something that I  realized is actually a big benefit of  doing work in where everything is  nationalized because the company  archives were also part of the State  Archives so I got some really rich  sources that are sometimes when were  difficult to get from private companies  um so did some work their work in  libraries and then again went to went to  Russia and did some more archival work  there and kind of went through their  public libraries and hunted down some  sources and so it's really most of it  most of its archival there's also some  newspapers and more and more popular  accounts as well so what are some of  your findings well I think the the most  basic one that sounds kind of obvious  but actually wasn't is that oil was  really important there is a view that it  wasn't important in fact the much of the  secondary literature especially the  Soviet literature that i came across is  all saying the oil didn't matter wasn't  important and why do you think that is i  think that it wasn't important  economically in the way that we tend to  associate oil now and we look at the oil  curse and oil resources and all these  ways that oil money drives things but i  think that in this context it wasn't the  oil money I think it was oil first oil  as a resource which is really sore  literally just fueling the feeling  economy fueling Red Army providing  kerosene because it's a different kind  of industry in 1920 then is today is  very different the industry that we see  today is much more like a post-world war  two phenomenon so at that time Russia is  still  still very poor mostly dependent on  kerosene and these kinds of things so  the big exports and super big  moneymakers would would be more grain in  these kinds of issues so there's a  fixation on on that in the literature  and not on oil and i think that i found  that oil actually especially in this  1920 1922 very early very formative  period just has an outsized place in  both domestic and foreign policy but  also beyond its use as a commodity it's  also its use in terms of trying to  negotiate on the world stage trying to  set a place for this a new radical state  through reestablishing basic  partnerships and business partnerships  and things that had been severed during  the war that it was through these other  channels the oil was very important and  remained so for it for quite a while out  of that time period ok so um what was  the role of oil in the formation of the  soviet union well um that is a big  question and it changes I think in the  beginning years it's that role of  literally as a resource as energy oil as  energy it's internally for the country  they weren't really exporting it  anywhere at that point or other where  other parts of the Soviet Union were  shipping it to yeah that's a good  question actually it is important  primarily as hard currency early on so  once the basic needs of the military are  met the military and actually that the  railroad some of the Russian railroads  were already running on oil products  once those basic needs were met then the  remainder of that was sent abroad for  hard currency and then that hard  currency is in turn busted into the  economy and in many ways it was used to  rebuild the coal industry which is an  interesting dynamic and actually looked  very counterintuitive and a lot of  people thought for a long time that this  was evidence of Russian backwardness  right the Soviets didn't understand the  economy they didn't understand how to  use anything but in fact they were  trying  to use these the hard currency from  things like oil in order to create a  domestic market for coal because the  economy was shattered the whole country  was shattered so it was using it in this  way that was not immediately obvious to  outsiders but why call in that oil coal  is cheaper it's much cheaper it has a  different kind of infrastructure and I  think the other big issue is it oil is  very capital intensive for one thing  machinery is very expensive equipment is  very expensive going through a big  technological boom at this time and they  didn't have access to or the means to  get this new capital infrastructure  which is something that they do start  doing but also they came to the  conclusion that oil made the selenium  more vulnerable that it was actually a  liability and it was a liability  primarily because of where it was  located it's located right on the border  region with Turkey and Iran and at that  time the British were very active in  that area and they looked at it as  making the borders much more insecure  and much more vulnerable to foreign  occupation which had happened several  times over the course of World War one  alright so ultimately what do you  conclude I ultimately conclude that  there is a lot of work left to be done  in this area I think it's a really  exciting area and I think that there are  route there's room for a lot more work  and a lot more scholars than just you  happily have some colleagues in this  area and that it is important it is  something that we need to go back in we  need to understand more of because it  explains a lot about the way that the  economy is built at that time period but  also going into World War two what sorts  of challenges the Soviet Union face and  didn't face because of the way the  industry had developed and I think it  also tells us a lot about the way that  oil works outside of the capitalist  system that they were in sort of a  costume to this idea of oil and the oil  curse and I think there's a very  different dynamic in the Soviet Union  that really needs to be  explored because I think the story is a  very different one very good very  interesting thank you very much for  being here with us today great thank you  for more information about professor  Brinegar and her work please visit our  website at macmillan report yale edu be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at gale  you
 

